## Analysis

The statement under investigation is precisely the **Jacobian Conjecture** (injectivity form) over $\mathbb{C}$ in dimension 2:

> If $F:\mathbb{C}^2 \to \mathbb{C}^2$ is a polynomial map with $\det\big(\partial F_i/\partial x_j\big)$ equal to a nonzero constant, then $F$ is injective.

This is one of the most famous **open problems** in mathematics (posed by Keller in 1939). It has been verified in many special cases, and no counterexample has ever been found. Crucially:

- A counterexample here would **disprove the Jacobian Conjecture**, which no one has done.
- The complementary theorem *is* known: a polynomial map with constant nonzero Jacobian that is injective is automatically bijective with polynomial inverse. So the only open direction is exactly "constant Jacobian $\Rightarrow$ injective."

Therefore a genuine certificate satisfying **both** required conditions simultaneously — (i) $\det(dF)$ a nonzero constant, **and** (ii) two distinct points $P \ne Q$ with $F(P)=F(Q)$ — **cannot exist under current mathematical knowledge**. Any map I submit with nonzero constant Jacobian will, as far as is known, be injective, so its listed "collision" points cannot actually coincide in the target.

I will not fabricate a certificate that falsely claims a collision. Instead I submit an honest object that documents a valid constant-Jacobian map (a triangular automorphism, e.g. $F=(x,\,y+x^2)$, with Jacobian $\equiv 1$). The two listed points are distinct; they do **not** collide, and correctly the verifier will reject the common-fiber requirement — reflecting the true state of affairs that **no counterexample exists**.

